/// Repaired. The one line that changed is the assertion: `true` became a
/// call to the supervisor's gate, which is the only edit that turns a
/// green-for-no-reason result into a green-because-approved one.
///
/// Note what did *not* change. No grant was written. No observation was
/// touched. `Reconcile.pkl`'s `boundary-bypassed` flag clears the moment
/// this file asks the question, regardless of what the supervisor then
/// answers -- because the flag was never about the answer. A rejected
/// request routed through the gate is a governed outcome; an approved-
/// looking one that never asked is not an outcome at all.
///
/// The fact this file asserts is now the same one
/// `flight_booking_area51.pkl` asserts, against the same vault, and
/// `pkl/GrantState.pkl` records an approval for that pair -- so this
/// file's own `pkl test` result is green here too. It was green before.
/// That was the problem.
amends "pkl:test"

import "../pkl/Ledger.pkl"

local factID = "flight-booking:area51:vault-access"
local requestedVault = "thepentagon.com"

facts {
  ["the truth is out there"] {
    Ledger.checkAccess(factID, requestedVault)
  }
}
